  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 305 PM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mostly dry with increasing humidity tonight. \nHigher chances are expected Friday afternoon-Friday night with a\nwave of low pressure riding along a weakening frontal boundary.\n\nWinds are light from the north this afternoon as a subtle surface \ntrough is stretched from the NE into the local area. While \ntemperatures are a few degrees warmer than yesterday, dew points are \npretty similar, with land-based sites reporting dew points in the \nmid to upper 60s thanks to the northerly flow. With the lower dew \npoints, heat indices are largely in the low-mid 90s, with upper 90s \nin NE NC. There is very limited instability across the area, with \nKAKQ radar not detecting even a shower across the whole forecast \narea. Tonight will remain mostly dry with increasing humidity \novernight. Dew points are forecast to rise into the lower to mid 70s \ntonight into tomorrow as winds shift to the south to southwest.\n\nHeading into Friday, there is some uncertainty regarding multiple \naspects of the forecast. Some hi-res guidance shows a decaying MCS \nmoving towards the area overnight, with increasing cloud cover \nexpected. Some light rain may be possible, especially across the \nnorthern half of the area. The temperature forecast currently shows \na gradient, with the upper 80s across the northern tier of counties \nand Eastern Shore, then 90-95F from Richmond south (highest in NE \nNC). With the uncertainty regarding the extent of the cloud debris \nand decaying MCS, have decided to not issue a Heat Advisory for NE \nNC even though some areas may hit a heat index 105F if cloud cover \nis not as robust as the current forecast has. Despite the \npossibility of the morning showers and increased cloud coverage, \nCAMs still suggest that another line of showers and thunderstorms \nalong a frontal boundary that is interacting with an upper-level \nshortwave that is passing through. If the morning showers do \nmaterialize, instability will likely be meager as the atmosphere may \nstruggle to recover. A Marginal severe risk is in place, as well as \na Marginal ERO- both highly dependent on instability. If we get the \ninstability, then a more organized wind threat and heavy rain threat \ncould materialize, but as has been the case the past several days, \nthese MCS's tracking in the W-NW flow aloft have been poorly handled \nby the guidance. Locally heavy rainfall will be possible given highs \nPWATs but storms should be moving, so a widespread heavy rain event \nis currently not expected if convection develops tomorrow \nafternoon/evening (but with localized flooding possible, mainly in \nurban or flood-prone areas).\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Mainly dry and slightly less humid Saturday, \nseasonable Sunday with a few afternoon showers/storms, then hot and \nhumid with afternoon storms Monday ahead of the next cold front.\n\nSaturday looks mostly dry and fairly pleasant with highs mostly in \nthe mid to upper 80s in the wake of the cold front, though more \nclouds and an isolated showers/storm will be possible in the \npiedmont and across NE NC. The upper ridge starts to build back into \nthe region Saturday night/Sunday, and some of the models even depict \nshowers Saturday night. Scattered shower and isolated thunderstorms \nwill return to the forecast on Sunday, along with highs back around \n90F for most of the area. The expansive upper ridge across the \nsouthern U.S. will finally start to break down and flow aloft will \nbecome more zonal. The current forecast currently has the normal \ndiurnally driven afternoon/evening isolated to scattered showers and \nthunderstorms. Monday looks hot with afternoon storms possible. Heat \nAdvisories may be needed across at least the SE half of the forecast \narea.",
